{
  "abstract": "We study directed strongly regular graphs with $t = \\lambda + 1$ from the Hobart–Brouwer table, which marks the open sets with $v \\le 110$. For such $t$, the matrix $B = A + I$ satisfies $B^2 = (\\lambda + 2 - \\mu)B + \\mu J$. Known bounds limit the size of a class of vertices with equal rows of $B$ from above, and double counting from below; together they give the necessary condition $(\\lambda + 2 - \\mu)^2 \\le \\mu(v - k - 1)$. It is invariant under blow-up by cliques and excludes the sets $(40, 9, 6, 5, 1)$, $(80, 19, 13, 12, 2)$ and $(96, 15, 10, 9, 1)$. When $\\mu = 1$ and $k = 2\\lambda + 2$, the rank of $B$ is $k + 2$, and the structure of a twinless vertex gives every vertex twins when $\\lambda \\ge 3$; when $\\lambda \\ge 4$, the classes are pairs, while a neighbourhood of $2\\lambda + 3$ vertices cannot be split into pairs. This closes $(66, 10, 5, 4, 1)$ and $(91, 12, 6, 5, 1)$. For $(28, 6, 3, 2, 1)$, a twinless vertex is excluded by the SAT solver CaDiCaL with a DRAT proof accepted by drat-trim and an LRAT checker, and without such a vertex all classes are pairs, while a neighbourhood of seven vertices cannot be split into pairs. The six sets are closed with their complements; for four of them nonexistence was known, and our proofs are different. Flag graphs of generalized quadrangles show that the bounds and the threshold $\\lambda \\ge 4$ are sharp. All exclusions except the case of a twinless vertex for $(28, 6, 3, 2, 1)$ are proved in Lean 4 with Mathlib for any matrix; no nonstandard axioms are used.",
  "author": "meleta",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 answers three comments of the review of version 1, corrects the attribution of the bounds on classes and moves the numbers to the new version of the table [2]; the proofs and the results are unchanged. (1) A direct comparison with Lemma 9 of [7] and Lemma 2 of [8], and the attribution of the bounds. The paragraph \"The bound on equal rows\" of Section 2 states the hypotheses of Lemma 9 of [7], shows that they hold for $B = A + I$ when $t = \\lambda + 1$ ($\\alpha = \\mu$, and $\\alpha + q < K$ is equivalent to $t < k$) and that the lemma gives exactly the bound $a \\le K - q$ of Proposition 10; Proposition 10 is called another proof of a known bound, and the difference between the arguments is stated. The rows of $A + I$ are equal exactly when the rows of the matrix $J - I - A$ of the complement are equal, so the bound $a \\le \\lambda + 1$ of Lemma 6 is the bound of our survey [4, Theorem 10(a)] applied to the complement; version 1 did not say this and counted Lemma 6 as ours. Now this is said in Section 1 (\"How the proof works\", contribution 1, \"What is taken from earlier work\"), in a new remark after Lemma 6 (the proof is kept: it is the same count in terms of $B$, and its inclusions are needed later) and in Section 2; Lemma 2 of [8] and the bound $4m - 1$ of its Section 7 are named as cases of the same bound for order $28m$. Contribution 1 is narrowed to Lemma 7, Propositions 8 and 9 and inequality (1). The paragraph \"Twins\" of Section 2 is rewritten: version 1 attributed to [8] and [9] the claim of a general bound for open neighbourhoods and contrasted them with closed ones; now the general bound and the quotient by classes when $t = \\mu$ are given by the survey [4, Theorem 10], and [8] is named as a work that already counts the rows of $A + I$. The last paragraph of Section 2 no longer says that the argument through classes and the rank is absent from [4]: the survey [4, Theorem 10(b)] compares the number of classes with the rank, and what we did not find is the structure of a vertex without a twin of Theorem 14; the same paragraph names Lemma 6 of [8], with the same argument on the number of classes of rows of $B$ and the rank. The paragraph \"The set of Theorem 4\" describes the method of [8] for order 28 from its text, not from the summary in the survey. A new paragraph of Section 2, \"Other local conditions\", sets apart the conditions of Hobart and Williford ([27, Theorems 3 and 6] and [27, Corollaries 4, 5 and 7]; the conditions N10 and N11 of the table) and shows that inequality (1) does not follow from their conditions on the parameters, and sets apart our paper 74; these are the new references [27] and [28]. The abstract calls the upper bounds on a class known. (2) Section 10 is shortened to a summary: three kinds of support (a proof by hand, a Lean theorem, a SAT certificate) and one or two sentences on each of Checks 1–7. The counts of equality cases, the outcomes of the spoilings and the account of the reviewers are moved, with the numbers unchanged, to a new section \"Checks in detail\" of the README of the supplement; that section also describes seven spoilings of Checks 5 and 6 that were earlier named only by their commands. After the proof of Theorem 14, Figure 1 is added with a caption and a description: the blocks $W_o$, the values $c_w$ and the subsets $T_o$ at one vertex of the graph $(6, 2, 1, 0, 1)$ and at two vertices of the graph $(15, 4, 2, 1, 1)$; a program of the supplement draws the figure and checks the claims of the proof at these vertices. (3) The LRAT proof of the first case of Theorem 4, 2,108,091 bytes and 89,248 compressed by xz, is in the supplement with a program that builds the formula anew, compares its SHA-256 hash and checks the proof without the solver; the formula itself, 6,395,464 bytes and 651,588 compressed, does not fit the limit of the supplement. Section 11 and a row of Table 6 say this. (4) The numbers follow the version of the table [2] of 31 October 2026, which marks $(40, 9, 6, 5, 1)$ and $(80, 19, 13, 12, 2)$ with their complements as nonexistent: Section 1 (24 open sets with $t = \\lambda + 1$, five of them with $\\mu = 1$), the paragraph of Section 2 on the sets of Theorem 2, contribution 1 and Corollary 11 (of the six excluded sets, one is open), and Section 9 (of the five open sets with $\\mu = 1$ the paper closes four; for $\\mu \\ge 2$ there are 19 open sets, and only $(81, 17, 10, 9, 2)$ is narrowed). The dated caveat about two versions of the table is removed, and the reference [2] names the new version. (5) Clarifications: Section 6 says that Theorem 14 applies to the set $(28, 6, 3, 2, 1)$ but its conclusion $\\lambda \\le 2$ holds; contribution 1 defines blow-up by cliques; Table 4 names the theorem `L94_cl_in` for Lemma 6 and the hypotheses of `L94_admissible` for Proposition 9, and calls the module of 13 theorems the module on the complement; Check 5 says that the vertex 0 of the second control graph has a row twin and refers to the SHA-256 hash of Section 11 instead of \"the first run\"; the sentence of the abstract on the axiom check is shortened to \"no nonstandard axioms are used\"; the proof of Theorem 14 defines the notation $B[w]$ for the row $w$ of $B$, and the vertices with $c_w = -1$ are denoted $M$, so as not to confuse them with $N^+[x]$. The supplement is rebuilt on the snapshot of the new version, and the run records that diverged from the output of the current programs are taken anew.",
